// The ruled circuit block form — `sign` blocks, kind-keyword members, the
// four-glyph arrow grid, two-sided named ports, and the `node` / `feed` body
// statements (parse-only fixture).
//
// GRADUATION: the ruled circuit RULE BLOCK has graduated -- a well-formed
// single-redex block lowers to its whiskered composite behind the cell layer's
// admission gate, and the runnable witness is
// examples/model/circuit/circuit-rule-block.gandr with its five declines under
// examples/pathological/circuit/. What stays here is what has NOT graduated:
// the reserved `<->` glyph (the reversible-oper lane), the `feed` wheel and the
// many-out node (both the cell-alphabet question, gandr-ui9), an `oper`
// member's filler (the 1-cell definition form), and a `rule` member with no
// filler (the sphere-typed declaration table, the higher-cells lane).
// Parse-only; never lowered.
//
// The arrow grid, in one line: the shaft carries the kind-class and the head
// carries directedness, so `-->` / `<->` are the circuit 1-cell formers and
// `==>` / `<=>` the rewrite faces at every dimension. The term language's `->`
// is untouched and disjoint.

// The congruence cell — one diagram with two disjoint redexes, which is the
// shape a term syntax cannot write because a term has one root. A `sign`
// block presents sorts, operations, and rules only: the item-level `data`
// member is retired (a family is declared once, whole, as a nested generator
// block — see `surface/data-declarations.gandr`), and a rule telescope's
// `data x : Nat` binders are a different slot, unaffected.
sign Nat {
  sort Nat : Type
  oper add : (Nat, Nat) --> Nat

  rule cong2 : (
    rule p : Nat ==> Nat,
    rule q : Nat ==> Nat,
    data x : Nat,
    data y : Nat
  ) ==> (z : Nat) {
    node : p(x) ==> (x′);
    node : q(y) ==> (y′);
    node : add(x′, y′) --> (z);
  }
}

// The first stateful wheel: `zip`'s `next` feeds back, one step later, as its
// own `state`. `feed` is the only statement that may close a cycle.
oper accumulate : (stream : Stream(Nat)) --> (out2 : Stream(Nat)) {
  node : zip(stream, state) --> (next, out2);
  feed : (next) --> (state);
}

// The sugar ladder written out: the operation in the named-port normal form
// the elaborator and the name-set fold see (unnamed tuple inputs mint fresh
// names in order).
sign NatDesugared {
  sort Nat : Type
  oper add : (l : Nat, r : Nat) --> (sum : Nat)
}

// Occurrence labels, the empty interface, and the pinned-endpoint binder.
sign Labelled {
  sort Nat : Type
  oper unit : () --> (out : Nat)
  oper step : (i : Nat) --> (o : Nat)

  rule twice : (rule p : x ==> x′, data x : Nat) ==> (o : Nat) {
    node w1 : p(x) ==> (m);
    node w2 : step(m) --> (o);
  }
}

// The invertible rewrite face `<=>` — the certificate algebra's
// unconditional-composition licence — beside the directed `==>`.
sign Invertible {
  sort Bit : Type
  oper flip : (b : Bit) --> (c : Bit)
  rule involutive : (b : Bit) <=> (c : Bit)
}

// RESERVED: `<->` on an oper is a semantic claim about the function (a
// data-level iso), whose checking discipline the reversible lane owes. It
// parses here and is declined until that lane lands.
sign Reserved {
  sort Bit : Type
  oper negate : (b : Bit) <-> (c : Bit)
}
